//
// A binding whose value is a BRANCH, one of whose arms projects out of a container, keeps the
// value that container held when the container is reassigned afterwards — the same answer the
// unbranched bind has always given (loft#1396).
//
//     x = if k > 0 { h.inner } else { mk(0) };
//     h = Hold{inner: mk(5), t: "k"};        // x still reads 1, not 5
//
// `(B-Disturb)` ends the place a view names when its container is reassigned, and `(B-View)`
// materialises a view live across that.  The two shapes this file pins were outside it for one
// reason: the walk that NAMES such views asked which container the value projects from, and
// asked it of the whole `if`, which projects from nothing.  So `x` was never named, and the
// first bind read the NEW container on both backends while the reassignment spelling read it on
// `--native` alone — an `(O-NoDiverge)` split on top of the wrong answer.
//
// The naming now looks THROUGH a branch to the container its arms project from.  What supplies
// the copy is per ARM and not per statement: `(O-Complete)` asks for the fact per binding and
// per PATH, and only the projecting arm needs a store of its own, so `Scopes::arm_bind` gives
// that arm the owned temp the arm-lift machinery already knows how to bind — the minting arm
// keeps its own store and the join reconciles them as it did.
//
// ⚠ The deps strip is deliberately NOT part of this.  Stripping a branch-valued binding's deps
// makes it the OWNER of a store it only views, and the emitters have no whole-statement copy to
// pair with that (`container_element_base` answers `None` for an `if`), so its scope-exit free
// would name the CONTAINER's store.  That was built, measured and backed out; the per-arm temp
// is what replaced it.
//
// @falsified-at: bd629983 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 2 assertion failures -> 0, native
//   exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failure -> 0.  (The two backends stop at different points,
//   which is why one counts two and the other one.)
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees. Falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on
//   both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

struct Pay { a: integer, b: text }
struct Hold { inner: Pay, t: text }

fn mk(n: integer) -> Pay { Pay{a: n, b: "n{n}"} }

// THE DEFECT — a FIRST bind from a branch whose then arm projects.
fn test_a_first_bind_from_a_projecting_arm() {
  h = Hold{inner: mk(1), t: "h"};
  k = 1;
  x = if k > 0 { h.inner } else { mk(0) };
  h = Hold{inner: mk(5), t: "k"};
  assert(x.a == 1, "the value the container held: got {x.a}, want 1");
}

// The REASSIGNMENT spelling, which was right on the interpreter and wrong on `--native`.
fn test_a_reassignment_from_a_projecting_arm() {
  h = Hold{inner: mk(1), t: "h"};
  k = 1;
  x = mk(0);
  assert(x.a == 0, "the first bind stands on its own, so the reassignment is one");
  x = if k > 0 { h.inner } else { mk(0) };
  h = Hold{inner: mk(5), t: "k"};
  assert(x.a == 1, "both backends agree: got {x.a}, want 1");
}

// The MINTING arm of the same branch is untouched — it owns its store and reads its own value.
fn test_the_minting_arm_is_unaffected() {
  h = Hold{inner: mk(1), t: "h"};
  k = 0;
  x = if k > 0 { h.inner } else { mk(7) };
  h = Hold{inner: mk(5), t: "k"};
  assert(x.a == 7, "the arm that mints: got {x.a}, want 7");
}

// The unbranched bind, which has answered this since @PLN130 F8 — the control that says the
// branch shapes were made to agree with it rather than given a rule of their own.
fn test_the_unbranched_bind_is_unchanged() {
  h = Hold{inner: mk(1), t: "h"};
  x = h.inner;
  h = Hold{inner: mk(5), t: "k"};
  assert(x.a == 1, "no branch: got {x.a}, want 1");
}

// CONTROL — an UNDISTURBED projection arm still ALIASES, which is what `(B-View)` is for.  A
// fix that copied every projection arm would pass every cell above and fail this one.
//
// ⚠ THIS CONTROL IS TAIL-SPECIFIC, and adding a collection cell to it in this form would
// assert the opposite of the language.  `(B-View)` makes a STRUCT-typed projection a view that
// aliases without `&` — the one place aliasing is the default — while `(B-View-Base)` puts a
// COLLECTION projection off an owned base on `(B-Copy)`, which COPIES: `p = t.proto; p += [9]`
// leaves `t.proto` at its old length, in this branch spelling exactly as in the plain one.  So
// for a vector tail the write reaching nothing IS the documented answer, and a control written
// the other way reports a correct fix as over-wide — which is what it did to loft#1399's fix,
// from a cell list of mine that did not qualify the tail.  The comparison that settles which
// kind a tail is: the PLAIN spelling of the SAME tail on the SAME build.
fn test_an_undisturbed_projection_arm_still_aliases() {
  h = Hold{inner: mk(1), t: "h"};
  k = 1;
  x = if k > 0 { h.inner } else { mk(0) };
  x.a = 9;
  assert(x.a == 9, "the view reads its own write: got {x.a}, want 9");
  assert(h.inner.a == 9, "and it reaches the container: got {h.inner.a}, want 9");
}

// CONTROL — a LOOP runs the branch again, so each pass reads the container as it then stands.
fn test_a_loop_reads_each_passs_own_container() {
  h = Hold{inner: mk(1), t: "h"};
  k = 1;
  got = 0;
  for i in 0..3 {
    x = if k > 0 { h.inner } else { mk(0) };
    h = Hold{inner: mk(i + 10), t: "k"};
    got = x.a;
  }
  assert(got == 11, "the third pass reads the second pass's container: got {got}, want 11");
}
